
# Crop Recommendation System — Fed-SwiFT

Full project: **PyTorch model training (Centralized + Federated) + Flask backend API + HTML/CSS/JS frontend**, structured so you can open it directly in VS Code and connect frontend ↔ backend easily.

All training logic (model architecture, hyperparameters, data split, FedAvg, Dirichlet Non-IID partitioning) is copied **exactly** from the original notebook — same seed (42), same config — so the accuracy already obtained (Centralized ≈98.18%, IID Fed-SwiFT ≈99.32%, Non-IID Fed-SwiFT ≈97.73%) will reproduce.

```text
crop-recommendation-system/
├── backend/
│   ├── data/
│   │   └── Crop_recommendation.csv      <-- YOU add this file
│   ├── models/                          <-- trained weights saved here
│   ├── results/                         <-- CSV metrics saved here
│   ├── src/
│   │   ├── config.py                    <-- all constants (seed, epochs, etc.)
│   │   ├── model.py                     <-- SwiFT architecture
│   │   ├── dataset.py                   <-- CropDataset
│   │   ├── data_pipeline.py             <-- load/encode/split/scale
│   │   ├── federated.py                 <-- FedAvg, partitions, client training
│   │   └── utils.py                     <-- train/evaluate loops
│   ├── train_centralized.py             <-- trains plain SwiFT
│   ├── train_federated_iid.py           <-- trains IID Fed-SwiFT
│   ├── train_federated_non_iid.py       <-- trains Non-IID Fed-SwiFT
│   ├── train_all.py                     <-- runs all three in sequence
│   ├── app.py                           <-- Flask API (serves predictions)
│   └── requirements.txt
└── frontend/
    ├── index.html                       <-- prediction form (plain HTML/JS)
    ├── css/style.css
    └── js/script.js                     <-- calls the Flask API
```

---

## Step 1 — Open the project in VS Code

1. Open VS Code → **File > Open Folder** → select `crop-recommendation-system`.
2. Install the **Python** extension in VS Code if you don't have it already.

---

## Step 2 — Add your dataset

Copy `Crop_recommendation.csv` (the same file you used in PyCharm) into:

```text
backend/data/Crop_recommendation.csv
```

---

## Step 3 — Create a virtual environment (VS Code terminal)

Open a terminal in VS Code (`Ctrl+``) and run:

```bash
cd backend
python -m venv venv
```

Activate it:

* **Windows (PowerShell):**

```powershell
venv\Scripts\Activate.ps1
```

* **Windows (cmd):**

```cmd
venv\Scripts\activate.bat
```

* **Mac/Linux:**

```bash
source venv/bin/activate
```

In VS Code, also select this interpreter:

`Ctrl+Shift+P` → **Python: Select Interpreter** → choose the one inside `backend/venv`.

---

## Step 4 — Install dependencies

```bash
pip install -r requirements.txt
```

> If you have a GPU and want CUDA-accelerated `torch`, install PyTorch separately first following the official PyTorch installation instructions, then run the command above — pip will skip torch since it's already installed.

---

## Step 5 — Train the models

You can train all three (Centralized, IID Fed-SwiFT, Non-IID Fed-SwiFT) in one go:

```bash
python train_all.py
```

Or run them individually if you only need one:

```bash
python train_centralized.py
python train_federated_iid.py
python train_federated_non_iid.py
```

Each script prints the exact same per-round/per-epoch logs you saw in the notebook, and saves:

* `models/scaler.pkl` and `models/label_encoder.pkl` (shared preprocessing)
* `models/centralized_swift.pth`
* `models/iid_fed_swift.pth` ← **this is the one the API uses by default**
* `models/non_iid_fed_swift.pth`

This step reproduces training — it will take a couple of minutes on CPU (same as PyCharm), longer for federated rounds.

---

## Step 6 — Start the backend API

Still inside `backend/` (with the venv active):

```bash
python app.py
```

You should see:

```text
Loaded model: iid_fed_swift.pth
 * Running on http://0.0.0.0:5000
```

Test it works by opening:

`http://127.0.0.1:5000/health`

in a browser — you should get:

```json
{"status": "ok", ...}
```

> **Which model gets served?** By default, `app.py` loads `models/iid_fed_swift.pth` (your best-performing model, 99.32% accuracy). To serve a different one, open `backend/src/config.py` and change `DEFAULT_MODEL_PATH`.

---

## Step 7 — Open the frontend

Open `frontend/index.html` directly in your browser (double-click it, or right-click → **Open with Live Server** if you have that VS Code extension installed).

Fill in the 7 fields:

* N
* P
* K
* Temperature
* Humidity
* pH
* Rainfall

and click **Recommend Crop** — it calls your Flask API at:

```text
http://127.0.0.1:5000/predict
```

and displays the predicted crop, the confidence %, and the top-3 alternatives.

> If your frontend and backend end up running on different machines/ports later, just update `API_BASE_URL` at the top of `frontend/js/script.js`.

---

## Step 8 — Plug this into your existing UI

Since you already built your own UI, you don't need to use `frontend/index.html` as-is — just reuse the fetch call from `frontend/js/script.js` inside your own page:

```javascript
fetch("http://127.0.0.1:5000/predict", {
  method: "POST",
  headers: { "Content-Type": "application/json" },
  body: JSON.stringify({
    N: 90, P: 42, K: 43,
    temperature: 20.87, humidity: 82.0,
    ph: 6.5, rainfall: 202.93
  })
})
  .then(res => res.json())
  .then(data => console.log(data));
// { predicted_crop: "rice", confidence: 99.1, top3: [...], model_used: "..." }
```

Wire this into whatever button/form you already built.

